<!--Post: 3440--><!--Time: 1104896881-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3440"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Q<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Q<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 04, 2005 - 7:48 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->BTK has made ref to &#34;Whichita Lineman&#34; the song in his &#34;you could call me...this&#34; letter.  <BR> <BR>He also has shown that he has and will use fake IDs. <BR> <BR>Charlie Otero said in a interview in 2004 that a Phone Company  Repairman knocked at the door, a few days before the killings. His Dad, Joseph made him go look out a window to make sure there was a Company truck in the driveway before he would open the door. <BR> <BR>Question: What &#34;was&#34; the reason for the phone company repairman to be there, at the Otero home? <BR> <BR>Has it been checked? <BR> <BR>Did... Mr.Otero open the door? <BR> <BR>Also, according to Charlie... the power went out in the days before and he had the family hide in a closet till he could look and see if the whole block was out... was it?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3440-->
<!--Post: 3550--><!--Time: 1104997566-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3550"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 05, 2005 - 11:46 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I agree - the lineman is intriguing.  Note that he actually suggested Wichita Hangman rather than Wichita Lineman &#40;I think that&#39;s what you intended to say&#41;.  I would think that Lineman would have been much more of a cruel joke, and would have gotten him far more infamy, but I think that was a little too close to home, so he passed on using the song title.  An interesting twist, that ties this to other threads, is that the phone &#34;problem&#34; could have been caused by BTK himself if he were a lineman.  And what better way to stalk than by phone?  Plus, he can cause a line problem whenever needed.  Company could even send him out on a legit call to the victim&#39;s house, and he gets automatic alibi.  Very slick.  But he could be any lineman - phone or electric &#40;since there needs to be some mutual technology familiarity&#41; - moonlighting in the victim&#39;s neighborhood while the other workers are involved somewhere else.  In any case, he would have to use any legit contact with a victim sparingly - maybe for only one victim.  After that, he would have to be more careful.  Doing legitimate work at two victims&#39; houses would/should throw up a huge red flag. <BR> <BR>Another aspect of this idea is that it would validate Charlie O&#39;s statments about his dad&#39;s nervousness.  What would make you more nervous than hearing somebody snooping your line?  Back in the old days it was crude, with clicks, echos, breathing, and everything you would need to frighten the heck out of a parent.  If Mr. Otero was hearing somebody listening in, he&#39;d get nervous as all get-out.  Might even call the phone company to send somebody out.  How incredibly ironic. <BR> <BR>What if the MO-ID ruse is just that - a fake ID he didn&#39;t need?  Like a copy of a fellow lineman&#39;s ID.  Easy to lift, steal, borrow, or copy. <BR> <BR>As you can see, your thoughts about the lineman open up a whole slew of possibilities - none of them good for the victims.  I don&#39;t know the answers to your specific questions, and have not seen the answers anywhere, but they are excellent questions.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3550-->
<!--Post: 3674--><!--Time: 1105124011-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3674"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Q<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Q<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 10:53 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Pax56, I meant he made reference to &#34;Wichita Lineman&#34; by calling it &#39;Wichita Hangman&#39; but you state it far more clearly than I did, for sure. <BR> <BR>Take a look at the &#34;Glen Campbell Lyrics&#34; thread I started, if you would. <a href="http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/messages/11/204.html?1103922951" target=_top>http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/messages/11/204.htm l?1103922951</a> <BR> <BR>While many songs could be twisted in interpatation to fit a scary scenario if we try... the fact that BTK made ref to the Jimmy Webb/ Glen Campbell song in seemingly a passing ref is key to me thinking it may mean something. Really... is anything this guy does... &#34;a passing ref&#34;? <BR> <BR>Above the huanting lines of Wichita Lineman put in context of these crime... look at &#34;Gentle On My Mind&#34;... downright frightning in this context Pax. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3674-->
<!--Post: 3676--><!--Time: 1105124530-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3676"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Q<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Q<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 11:02 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The question remains... Why was phone repairman at the Otero Home, in the first place. Perhaps Tom can ask Charlie Otero if he recalls why or if Charlie... you read this please let me/us know. <BR> <BR>Interesting that Wichita was hit, this week, with an icestorm so bad it was on Fox News Channel. It&#39;s reported that up to 1/2 of Wichita is without power, as of yesterday and it may take up to a week to restore it. <BR> <BR>Now... BTK is probably to old and retired if he was a &#34;lineman&#34; but what a prime op. to be lurking in peoples yards and maybe more ironic maybe the very cover he used will bring ligit &#34;repairmen&#34; to his home if he lives in Wichita.Maybe an alert person in or around his home will spot something. <BR> <BR>I know this... old or not it has to give people a pause, at the very least to know that this guy is out there and the lights are out and maybe the phones are too... <BR> <BR>Scary.  <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3676-->
<!--Post: 4194--><!--Time: 1105847602-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4194"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bluepete<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bluepete<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 15, 2005 - 7:53 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->this is an excellent thread with still no answer<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4194-->
<!--Post: 5793--><!--Time: 1107379858-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5793"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Sjforcum<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Sjforcum<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, February 02, 2005 - 1:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Sorry if this has already been discredited or brought up...but could this ID play any role? <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.crimelibrary.com/serial_killers/unsolved/btk/12.html?sect=4" target=_top>http://www.crimelibrary.com/serial_killers/unsolve d/btk/12.html?sect=4</a> <BR> <BR>About half way down the page there is a referrence to a South West Bell ID being sent KAKE on May 5, 2004.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5793-->
<!--Post: 5816--><!--Time: 1107390776-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5816"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jim44095<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jim44095<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, February 02, 2005 - 4:32 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Yes sjforcum,it has been talked about,but it bears repeating.Somehow this guy got into homes either under the pretext of a repairman or an education officer.Maybe a truant officer?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5816-->
<!--Post: 8360--><!--Time: 1109092389-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8360"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Prairie_chicken<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Prairie_chicken<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 22, 2005 - 9:13 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->In answer to the first post in this thread, remember the Oteros had only lived in their home a short time.  In 1974 the phone company would come out to your house and hard wire the phones where you wanted them.  The phones actually belonged to the phone company and there were no phone jacks as we have now, you couldn&#39;t move them but a few feet, if the cord was long enough.  My guess is that after being in the home for a while, they had requested another phone be installed. <BR> <BR>Another option would be:  If you look at the weather in the first two weeks of January 1974, there were ice and snow storms, and extremely low temperatures, nightly lows were -5 to 10 degrees.  In that neighborhood, as many still are today, the phone lines extend from a telephone pole in the back and hook up to the nearest part of the house to the pole.  It&#39;s possible the severe weather had damaged phone lines in the area, requiring service.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8360-->
